The 4 October export describes [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) as one known issue with a fix queued.
Its reports were merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
The desk's classification code identifies two causes: parser and hint. Each has a ticket of its own.
The one-day reports remain an unresolved observation within the parser count.

## Engineering update

The [challenge page](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows), checked on 29 October,
reports that #4471 closed on 5 October:

- **Parser — fixed:** “fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales”.
  [Issue #1](https://github.com/hops-on-pop/mega-dev/issues/1) is closed as completed.
- **Hint — won't fix:** booking-v3 was retired.
  [Issue #2](https://github.com/hops-on-pop/mega-dev/issues/2) is closed as not planned.

<Callout kind="warn">
The update does not establish a separate cause or a verified fix for the one-day reports.
<a href="https://github.com/hops-on-pop/mega-dev/issues/3">Issue #3</a> remains open for investigation.
</Callout>

## What the export holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} repo="hops-on-pop/mega-dev" tickets={{ parser: 1, hint: 2, 'one-day': 3 }} />

## How the desk classified the reports

- **Parser:** the shared parser read dates month first while the tenants wrote day first.
  This could swap the day and month. Engineering reports this parser bug fixed.
- **Hint:** booking-v3 printed `MM/DD/YYYY`, while the form read dates day first.
  This was a different cause of swapped dates. The form was retired rather than fixed.
- **One-day observation:** the desk noted reports about dates moving by one day in `desk-notes.json`.
  The classification code includes them under parser by tenant. Their symptoms warrant investigation,
  but neither the export nor the engineering update confirms a distinct root cause or their resolution.

Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. The table uses the desk's tenant and channel
rules, deduplicates by report ID, and preserves the export's counts.

## Lessons

- Read the computed views alongside the prose. A shared subject does not establish a shared cause.
- Keep anomalous reports visible without promoting an unexplained symptom into a confirmed cause.
- Check current engineering updates before closing tickets. Fixed and won't fix require different closure reasons.

